<div class="category">VEGETABLE STORAGE</div>

<h1>How to Keep Vegetables Fresh for Longer</h1>

<p class="intro">
Buying fresh vegetables is only half the job. Knowing how to store them
properly can help maintain their quality, texture and flavour for longer.
</p>

<p>
Vegetables can spoil quickly when they are exposed to too much moisture,
heat, air or unsuitable storage conditions. The good news is that you do
not need complicated equipment to improve the way you store most vegetables
at home.
</p>

<p>
Different vegetables have different storage needs. Leafy greens usually
benefit from cool conditions and controlled moisture, while potatoes,
onions and garlic generally prefer a cool, dry and well-ventilated place.
</p>

<h2>1. Store Vegetables According to Their Needs</h2>

<p>
One of the most common storage mistakes is putting every vegetable in the
same place. Vegetables differ in how they respond to temperature and
humidity.
</p>

<p>
Before storing your vegetables, separate them into groups such as leafy
greens, root vegetables, vegetables that need refrigeration and vegetables
that prefer a dry pantry.
</p>

<div class="tip">
<strong>Simple tip:</strong> Do not automatically refrigerate every
vegetable. Some vegetables keep better in a cool, dry and dark place.
</div>

<h2>2. Keep Leafy Greens Fresh</h2>

<p>
Leafy vegetables such as spinach, lettuce and similar greens can lose their
freshness quickly. Excess water on the leaves can also encourage spoilage.
</p>

<p>
Remove damaged leaves first. If the greens are wet, gently dry them before
storage. You can place them in a clean container or bag with a paper towel
to help manage excess moisture.
</p>

<h2>3. How to Store Carrots</h2>

<p>
Carrots generally keep well in the refrigerator. Before storing them,
remove leafy tops if they are still attached because the greens can draw
moisture from the carrot.
</p>

<p>
Keep carrots in a suitable container or produce bag. Avoid storing them
while covered in excess water.
</p>

<h2>4. Store Broccoli Properly</h2>

<p>
Broccoli is best kept cool. Refrigeration can help maintain its quality
for longer than leaving it at room temperature.
</p>

<p>
Avoid washing broccoli long before you plan to use it unless you can dry
it thoroughly. Excess moisture can contribute to faster deterioration.
</p>

<h2>5. Keep Potatoes in a Cool, Dark Place</h2>

<p>
Potatoes are different from many vegetables because they generally do not
need refrigerator storage. A cool, dark and well-ventilated location is
usually more suitable.
</p>

<p>
Keep potatoes away from direct sunlight. Light can cause potatoes to
develop green areas and may affect their quality.
</p>

<h2>6. How to Store Onions</h2>

<p>
Whole onions usually prefer a dry, cool and well-ventilated place.
Avoid keeping them in a sealed container where air cannot circulate.
</p>

<p>
It is also useful to keep onions away from vegetables that produce a lot
of moisture.
</p>

<h2>7. Do Not Wash Everything Before Storage</h2>

<p>
Washing vegetables before storage may seem convenient, but excess moisture
can sometimes shorten their storage life.
</p>

<p>
For many vegetables, it is better to wash them shortly before cooking or
eating. If you do wash them before storage, make sure they are properly
dried first.
</p>

<h2>8. Use Your Refrigerator Correctly</h2>

<p>
Your refrigerator has different areas with different temperature and
humidity conditions. Use the vegetable or produce drawer when appropriate.
</p>

<p>
Avoid packing the drawer so tightly that air cannot circulate. Good
organization makes it easier to see what you have and use older vegetables
before buying more.
</p>

<h2>9. Keep Spoiled Vegetables Separate</h2>

<p>
Check your vegetables regularly. If one item has clearly spoiled, remove
it from the storage area so it does not affect your ability to monitor the
remaining produce.
</p>

<p>
Also remove damaged or badly bruised pieces when appropriate. Small
damaged areas can deteriorate faster than healthy parts.
</p>

<h2>10. Buy Only What You Can Use</h2>

<p>
One of the easiest ways to reduce vegetable waste is to avoid buying much
more than you can reasonably use.
</p>

<p>
Planning a few meals before shopping can help you choose the right
quantity. You can also prioritise vegetables that are more perishable and
save longer-lasting vegetables for later.
</p>

<h2>Quick Vegetable Storage Guide</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Leafy greens:</strong> Keep cool and manage excess moisture.</li>
  <li><strong>Carrots:</strong> Refrigerate and keep reasonably dry.</li>
  <li><strong>Broccoli:</strong> Keep refrigerated for better freshness.</li>
  <li><strong>Potatoes:</strong> Store in a cool, dark and ventilated place.</li>
  <li><strong>Onions:</strong> Keep dry and well ventilated.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

<p>
Keeping vegetables fresh does not require expensive equipment. The most
important steps are understanding the storage needs of each vegetable,
controlling excess moisture, avoiding unsuitable temperatures and checking
your produce regularly.
</p>

<p>
With a few simple habits, you can improve the quality of the vegetables in
your kitchen and reduce unnecessary food waste.
</p>

